        "title": "Cage vs POD(Point of Deployment)",
        "excerpt":"Cage     Definition: A physical security barrier, typically a wire-mesh fence or partition.   Purpose: Security &amp; Access Control. Used to physically secure a tenant’s racks from others.   Common Use: Co-location (multi-tenant) data centers.   Scope: An enclosure around one or more racks.     POD (Point of Deployment)      Definition: A logical, standardized, and repeatable “building block” of infrastructure.   Purpose: Scalability &amp; Standardization. Used to rapidly deploy new capacity.   Common Use: Large-scale cloud and enterprise data centers.   Scope: A pre-defined set of racks, network switches, servers, and power. (e.g., “1 POD = 20 Racks + 2 Leaf Switches”).     Summary: Cage vs. POD                  Attribute       Cage       POD                       Concept       Physical Security       Logical Design                 Main Goal       Access Control       Scalability                 Represents       A “pen” or secure area       A “stamp” or “Lego block”                 Context       Co-location / Multi-Tenant       Architecture / Deployment          ","categories": ["IT Infrastructure Engineering"],
